  "worked_solution": "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+P(x){y}^{\u2032}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{\u2033}+x{y}^{\u2032}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
